  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA few showers are starting to move into northeast Nebraska along\nthe cold front as it pushes into Knox County. As this front\nmoves through we'll see winds shift to northerly and scattered \nshowers spread across the area. While model soundings show \ndecent shear (35-50kt) behind the front, instability will be \nlimited making it difficult for us to see much more than a few \nlightning strikes with our strongest storms overnight. \n\nLooking back at the larger picture we have a cold front moving \nthrough, stemming from a longwave trough located over central \nCanada. This cold front has brought a cooler Canadian air mass \nsouth into the Northern Plains. The cold front looks to stall \nacross our area tomorrow. Showers and storms trigger along this \nboundary as weak shortwaves move across our area. Showers will \nlikely remain fairly scattered through the morning into the \nafternoon due to weak forcing. PWAT values north of our 90th \npercentile may allow for some locally heavy rainfall from these \nshowers and storms, but they should move well enough to not be \nmuch of a threat for flash flooding. \n\nA more substantial shortwave moves through tomorrow night into\nWednesday bringing greater coverage of showers and storms. \nAgain, we'll lack enough instability for much of a severe \nweather threat overnight, but ample moisture as well as \npotential for training storms along the frontal boundary could \nlead to greater potential for flash flooding into Wednesday\nmorning. We'll get a better idea of the exact corridor for \nheavier rainfall once the front stalls this afternoon, but it \nshould land fairly close to our forecast right now for areas \nalong and just south of the I-80 corridor. Highest rainfall \namounts through Wednesday look to be mostly over our southwest \nIowa counties which could see around 1-2 inches of rain. Areas \nalong the heavier rain axis in Nebraska will more likely see \nanywhere from 0.5-1.25 inches. Locally higher amounts will be \npossible, especially if we see any training storms.\n\nShowers and storms will taper off through the afternoon, ending\nby around 7pm Wednesday evening. With cloud cover and rain\nthrough much of the day, highs on Wednesday look to only reach\ninto the 70s. Even areas in northeast Nebraska that likely \nwon't see much rain at all will see highs stay in the mid-to-\nupper 70s due to the Canadian air mass in place. \n\nThursday we see one more vort max round the base of the upper-\nlevel trough across our area before it slides off to the east.\nThis could bring a few light showers Thursday evening, but our \nair mass should be too dry for much to reach the ground at that \npoint. Temperatures will stay fairly mild with mostly clear \nskies, only reaching into the low 80s Thursday afternoon. \n\nGoing into the weekend, the ridge over the Desert Southwest\nexpands eastward, filling in behind the departing trough. This\nwill allow temperatures to warm back up with highs in the upper\n80s on Friday and low-to-mid 90s on Saturday. Not expecting any\nrain through the weekend, but will start watching for rain\nchances returning possibly as early as Sunday night into early\nMonday, but more likely Monday night into Tuesday.",
